Subject: [PATCH 41/43] [3.1.1] [M156] Handle re-entrancy in P2PSocketManager teardown

Original change's description:
> Handle re-entrancy in P2PSocketManager teardown
>
> Destroying a P2P socket or pending DNS request cancels its underlying
> HostResolver request, which can synchronously fail queued sibling
> requests (for example when closing the last active DoH stream on a
> stalled socket pool). In ~P2PSocketManager, those failures re-entered
> P2PSocketManager::DestroySocket or P2PSocketManager::OnAddressResolved
> while sockets_ or dns_requests_ was being cleared.
>
> - In ~P2PSocketManager, stop sockets and DNS requests from calling back
>   into the manager before destroying them: invalidate the weak pointers
>   that DNS request callbacks are bound to, and clear each socket's
>   delegate (new P2PSocket::ClearDelegate()).
> - Move owned entries out of their containers before erasing in
>   P2PSocketManager::OnAddressResolved and
>   NetworkContext::DestroySocketManager.
> - Mark P2PSocketManager, P2PSocketUdp, P2PSocketTcp and
>   P2PSocketStunTcp final.
> - Add tests for the re-entrant paths, including the DestroySocket() fix
>   from crrev.com/c/8692125.
>
